from pathlib import Path
import json, posixpath

ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
BASE=ROOT/'Class 9'/'English Medium'/'English'
for d in ('Chapters','Concepts','Definitions','Exercises'):(BASE/d).mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
books={
1:{'title':'English Kerala Reader Standard IX Part I','overview':'Subject Overview Part 1.md','canvas':'English Part 1.canvas','units':[
('Hours and Years','7-40','This unit considers memory, time and gratitude through a short story, a personal essay and a poem. The reading sequence pairs the child narrator’s encounter with adult life in Half a Day, a reflective account of gratitude in Debts of Gratitude, and a poem about the irreversibility of time in Nothing Twice. The page design combines illustrations with response boxes, character mapping, narrative-sequence work, vocabulary, grammar and personal writing.'),
('Winds of Change','41-70','The unit follows change through social action and personal responsibility. Dreams Realised presents Gandhi’s work and ideals through narrative and biographical detail; The Toilet uses a first-person child’s account to examine sanitation, dignity and community action; Success is a poem about effort and perseverance. Activities move from comprehension to report writing, interviews, passive constructions, reported speech, adjective formation and public speaking.'),
('Aspire and Explore','71-109','This unit connects exploration, courage and resilience. Sea-Fever expresses a speaker’s attraction to the sea and travel; The Saga of the Tiffin Carriers describes Mumbai’s dabbawalas and the system behind reliable delivery; Waiting for Rain presents a Kerala family’s experience of a monsoon flood. The activities inspect sound and sensory imagery, narrative viewpoint, information reporting, homophones, relative clauses, suggestions, and descriptive writing. Illustrations and diagrams support the poem, delivery system and flood narrative.')],
'works':[
('Half a Day','Hours and Years','8-16','Naguib Mahfouz’s first-person story follows a child on his first school day. The initially strange school becomes a place of relationships and learning; when the narrator leaves, the world outside has changed and he discovers that a lifetime has passed. The movement from childhood to old age makes time, memory, social change and the brevity of life central. The textbook asks readers to track setting, attitude, character and narrative shifts.'),
('Debts of Gratitude','Hours and Years','17-21','The text reflects on acts of kindness and the lasting obligation to recognise them. Its first-person recollection links personal gratitude with memory, respect and generosity. The illustration and response questions prompt learners to distinguish the event from the narrator’s later reflection and to express gratitude in their own writing.'),
('Nothing Twice','Hours and Years','22-24','Wisława Szymborska’s poem reflects on time’s irreversibility: each moment and experience occurs once, so choices and encounters cannot be exactly repeated. Repeated phrasing and contrast support a reflective tone. The accompanying activities ask learners to discuss the speaker, repetition, mood and imagery rather than treat the poem as a set of literal instructions.'),
('Dreams Realised','Winds of Change','43-50','Mahadev Desai’s account presents Gandhi’s South African work and the development of satyagraha through narrative detail. The text links moral courage and non-violent resistance with organisation, self-discipline, service and social reform. Photographs and contextual notes anchor the account historically; activities practise sequencing, interpretation, report writing and public speech.'),
('The Toilet','Winds of Change','51-59','Gita Mirchandani’s first-person narrative describes a child’s experience of inadequate toilet facilities and the family’s attempts to respond. The narrator’s embarrassment and distress reveal how sanitation affects dignity, health, education and equal participation. The story moves from a personal problem toward communication and civic responsibility; activities ask readers to consider access, voice, solutions and the story’s perspective.'),
('Success','Winds of Change','60-63','Henry Wadsworth Longfellow’s poem considers how achievement is remembered and why effort matters. Its reflective voice invites learners to distinguish public recognition from sustained work and to notice repetition and contrast. Questions connect the poem’s idea of success with everyday experience.'),
('Sea-Fever','Aspire and Explore','73-77','John Masefield’s lyric voice longs for a return to the sea. Repeated openings create the rhythm of a wish; nautical vocabulary, movement, weather and sound imagery evoke a sailor’s life. Activities identify sensory images and poetic devices and recast the poem’s journey as a choreographic or descriptive response.'),
('The Saga of the Tiffin Carriers','Aspire and Explore','78-84','Hugh and Colleen Gantzer describe Mumbai’s dabbawalas: a large, coordinated service that collects, sorts, transports and returns home-cooked meals. The account explains the human organisation and delivery chain, the system’s distinctive coding and reliability, and the discipline and cooperation behind it. Photographs and diagrams clarify the route and work. Tasks include report/news writing and process explanation.'),
('Waiting for Rain','Aspire and Explore','85-109','Anita Nair’s story centres on a family and village during heavy monsoon rain and flooding in Kerala. The child narrator records fear, uncertainty, practical response and community care as water disrupts ordinary life. Rain is both a physical danger and an emotional presence. The textbook’s illustrations, dialogue and sensory detail support close reading; follow-up work covers point of view, sequence, vocabulary, homophones and conditional language.') ]},
2:{'title':'English Kerala Reader Standard IX Part II','overview':'Subject Overview Part 2.md','canvas':'English Part 2.canvas','units':[
('Strive and Thrive','119-154','This unit explores persistence, courage and self-reliance through a personal narrative, a report on an act of courage and a poem. The Unstoppable Soul Surfer recounts Bethany Hamilton’s return to surfing after a shark attack; A Shield of Courage profiles Neerja Bhanot and the hijacking of Pan Am Flight 73; Walk Alone presents a call to continue along a chosen path. Activities develop interview writing, report structure, character analysis, imagery and public speaking.'),
('Media Matters','155-180','This unit examines how media shape choices, identity and culture. Toys to Teens discusses advertising directed at children and the pressure to consume; The Ink and the Melody explores childhood, popular culture, music and a young person’s relationship with an iconic performer; Let It Go uses a song to discuss self-expression and emotional release. The unit also develops media literacy, privacy and cyber safety, advertisement analysis, reported speech, clauses, homophones, persuasive language and survey work.')],
'works':[
('The Unstoppable Soul Surfer','Strive and Thrive','121-131','Bethany Hamilton’s autobiographical account describes a shark attack that cost her an arm and her determination to return to surfing. The narrative follows shock, recovery, adaptation and renewed participation, showing the role of family, faith, training and self-belief. Photographs and captions connect the prose to real events. Activities distinguish fact from reflection, develop a character profile and prepare an interview or newspaper report.'),
('A Shield of Courage','Strive and Thrive','132-138','The account profiles Neerja Bhanot’s actions as a flight attendant during the hijacking of Pan Am Flight 73 in 1986. It describes how she helped passengers under threat and died while enabling others to escape. Chronology, reporting and biographical details explain the courage and responsibility involved; the accompanying activities practise report structure, interview questions, speeches and discussion of ethical action.'),
('Walk Alone','Strive and Thrive','139-154','Rabindranath Tagore’s poem urges the listener to continue a principled path when support or recognition is absent. The repeated refrain, imperative voice and images of light, sound and solitary movement create resolve. The translation is paired with activities on poetic devices, imagery, resilience and the poem’s contemporary relevance.'),
('Toys to Teens','Media Matters','157-165','The article examines how advertising targets children and adolescents, shapes desires and links products with popularity or identity. It considers media strategies and the influence of marketing on family choices and consumption. The illustrated examples invite learners to analyse audience, purpose, persuasive claims and visual design, then create or critique advertisements using evidence and ethical judgment.'),
('The Ink and the Melody','Media Matters','166-168','The narrative explores a young person’s interest in music and a famous performer, connecting popular culture with family relationships and childhood imagination. Dialogue and changing viewpoints reveal how music can influence identity and shared experience. The activities ask learners to interpret character motivation, distinguish fact from opinion and discuss how media personalities affect young audiences.'),
('Let It Go','Media Matters','169-180','The song from Frozen presents a speaker releasing fear and constraint and claiming a more independent identity. Repetition, imagery of cold and transformation, and shifts in voice carry the emotional change. The textbook pairs the lyrics with listening, interpretation, poetic-device and media questions; it also includes language practice and a project on advertising and youth culture.') ]}}

definitions=[
('Sensory imagery','Sea-Fever','73-77','Language that evokes sight, sound, touch, taste or smell. In Sea-Fever the sea, wind, sail, call and movement create an imagined voyage.'),
('Onomatopoeia','Aspire and Explore','91-96','A word whose sound imitates or suggests the sound it names. The unit asks learners to notice sound-imitating forms in a narrative and comic-style examples.'),
('Active and passive voice','Winds of Change','62-65','In active voice, the grammatical subject performs the action; in passive voice, the subject receives it. A passive form uses an appropriate form of “be” plus a past participle; the agent may be included with “by” or omitted when unknown or unimportant.'),
('Reported speech','Winds of Change','64-66','Reported speech conveys another speaker’s words without quoting them directly. Pronouns, tense, time expressions and word order may shift to fit the reporting context; questions and commands require appropriate reporting structures.'),
('Relative clause','Aspire and Explore','97-101','A clause that modifies a noun by identifying or adding information about it, often introduced by who, which, that, whose or where. A defining clause identifies the noun; a non-defining clause adds parenthetical information and is set off by commas.'),
('Adjective and adverb formation','Hours and Years','32-36','Suffixes can form adjectives or adverbs and may change a word’s meaning or grammatical role. Choose the form that fits the sentence; spelling can change when suffixes are added.'),
('Persuasive advertisement','Media Matters','44-52','An advertisement is a designed message intended to influence an audience. Analyse the target group, claim, image, emotional appeal, evidence and omissions; consider privacy, stereotypes and the difference between information and persuasion.'),
('Poetic repetition and refrain','Strive and Thrive','139-144','Repetition is the recurrence of words, sounds or structures for emphasis and rhythm. A refrain is a recurring line or phrase; in Walk Alone it reinforces the central call to persist.'),
]
exercises={1:[('Hours and Years — Reading and Language Activities','Hours and Years','8-40','Activities cover narrative sequence and setting, a character map for Half a Day, reflective response to gratitude, imagery and repetition in Nothing Twice, vocabulary and word formation, narrative writing, and oral discussion. Responses should cite textual details; the book does not provide an answer key.'),('Winds of Change — Reading, Speaking and Writing','Winds of Change','43-70','Activities include comprehension of Gandhi’s work, a report or speech, interpreting the child narrator’s experience in The Toilet, comparing viewpoints, active/passive transformations, reported speech, adjective/suffix tasks, vocabulary and public-speaking practice.'),('Aspire and Explore — Poetry, Narrative and Language','Aspire and Explore','73-109','Tasks include sensory imagery and sound in Sea-Fever, process/report writing about the dabbawalas, narrative analysis and response to Waiting for Rain, homophones and relative clauses, diary or descriptive writing, suggestions and conditional expressions.')],2:[('Strive and Thrive — Courage and Resilience','Strive and Thrive','121-154','Activities ask learners to interpret the surfer’s recovery, develop a character sketch and interview, write reports and speeches about Neerja Bhanot, and analyse imagery, repetition and message in Walk Alone. Keep historical information distinct from personal interpretation.'),('Media Matters — Advertising, Music and Digital Citizenship','Media Matters','157-180','Activities analyse advertisements and their intended audiences, discuss children’s privacy and online safety, interpret narrative and song, transform direct to reported speech, practise clauses and language forms, and conduct a survey or media project.') ]}

def write(folder,name,kind,book,chapter,pages,body,related=()):
    p=BASE/folder/(name+'.md'); part=book; tag=kind.lower()
    refs='\n'.join(f'- [[{posixpath.relpath(r,folder)}]]' for r in related) or f'- [[{posixpath.relpath("Subject Overview Part "+str(part),folder)}]]'
    p.write_text(f'''---\nclass: 9\nmedium: English\nsubject: English\npart: {part}\nchapter: "{chapter}"\ntopic: "{name}"\ntype: {tag}\ntextbook: {books[part]['title']}\nprinted_pages: "{pages}"\ntags: [class/9, medium/english, subject/english, type/{tag}]\n---\n# {name}\n\n{body}\n\n**Textbook reference:** {books[part]['title']}, printed pp. {pages}.\n\n## Related notes\n{refs}\n''',encoding='utf-8'); return p

for part,b in books.items():
    nodes=[('Subject Overview Part '+str(part)+'.md',5)]; unit_nodes={}; node_paths={}; chapter_paths=[]
    for i,(title,pages,body) in enumerate(b['units'],1):
        name=f'Unit {i:02d} - {title}'; unit_nodes[title]=f'n{i+1}'
        p=write('Chapters',name,'chapter',part,title,pages,body,[f'Concepts/{w[0]}' for w in b['works'] if w[1]==title]+[f'Exercises/{e[0]}' for e in exercises[part] if e[1]==title])
        rel=f'Chapters/{name}.md'; chapter_paths.append(rel); nodes.append((rel,5))
    for title,unit,pages,body in b['works']:
        p=write('Concepts',title,'concept',part,unit,pages,body,[f'Chapters/Unit {i:02d} - {unit}' for i,(u,_,_) in enumerate(b['units'],1) if u==unit]); nodes.append((f'Concepts/{title}.md',1))
    for title,unit,pages,body in definitions:
        if part==1 and unit in ('Sea-Fever','Aspire and Explore','Winds of Change','Hours and Years'):
            # Several definitions belong to one volume; retain only exact in-volume page ranges.
            if (unit in ('Sea-Fever','Aspire and Explore') and pages.startswith('73')) or (unit=='Winds of Change' and pages.startswith('62')) or (unit=='Hours and Years' and pages.startswith('32')):
                p=write('Definitions',title,'definition',part,unit,pages,body,[f'Chapters/Unit {i:02d} - {unit}' for i,(u,_,_) in enumerate(b['units'],1) if u==unit]); nodes.append((f'Definitions/{title}.md',2))
        elif part==2 and unit=='Strive and Thrive':
            p=write('Definitions',title,'definition',part,unit,pages,body,[f'Chapters/Unit 01 - {unit}']); nodes.append((f'Definitions/{title}.md',2))
    for title,unit,pages,body in exercises[part]:
        p=write('Exercises',title,'exercise',part,unit,pages,body,[f'Chapters/Unit {i:02d} - {unit}' for i,(u,_,_) in enumerate(b['units'],1) if u==unit]); nodes.append((f'Exercises/{title}.md',4))
    # Overview and textbook map use only file nodes. Shared subject overview links the two volumes.
    chapter_links=''.join(f'- [[{path}|{title}]] — printed pp. {pages}\n' for path,(title,pages,_) in zip(chapter_paths,b['units']))
    overview=f'''---\nclass: 9\nmedium: English\nsubject: English\npart: {part}\nedition: First Edition 2024\ntype: overview\ntags: [class/9, medium/english, subject/english, type/overview]\n---\n# English — Part {part}\n\n**Kerala SCERT {b['title']}, First Edition 2024.** All pages were visually reviewed, including the reading texts, illustrations, response panels, language tables, writing tasks, glossary, note pages and civic inserts. The notes summarize the lessons and language learning without reproducing the textbook texts.\n\n## Units\n{chapter_links}\n## Colour legend\n- Red `#FF6B6B`: important concepts and reading ideas\n- Orange `#F4A261`: literary and language terms\n- Yellow `#E9C46A`: language patterns and rules\n- Green `#2A9D8F`: examples and activities\n- Blue `#457B9D`: units and main topics\n- Purple `#9B5DE5`: cross-unit connections\n\nOpen [[{b['canvas']}]] for the file-node map.\n'''
    (BASE/b['overview']).write_text(overview,encoding='utf-8')
    overview_file=b['overview']; allnodes=[(overview_file,5)]+nodes[1:]; canv_nodes=[]
    for i,(file,col) in enumerate(allnodes): canv_nodes.append({'id':f'n{i+1}','type':'file','file':file,'x':(i%3)*500,'y':(i//3)*180,'width':360,'height':130,'color':str(col)})
    edges=[]
    for i in range(len(b['units'])):edges.append({'id':f'e{i+1}','fromNode':'n1','fromSide':'right','toNode':f'n{i+2}','toSide':'left','color':'5'})
    lookup={n['file']:n['id'] for n in canv_nodes}
    for title,unit,_,_ in b['works']:
        unit_i=next(i for i,(u,_,_) in enumerate(b['units'],1) if u==unit); edges.append({'id':f'e{len(edges)+1}','fromNode':f'n{unit_i+1}','fromSide':'right','toNode':lookup[f'Concepts/{title}.md'],'toSide':'left','color':'5'})
    for title,unit,_,_ in exercises[part]:
        unit_i=next(i for i,(u,_,_) in enumerate(b['units'],1) if u==unit); edges.append({'id':f'e{len(edges)+1}','fromNode':f'n{unit_i+1}','fromSide':'right','toNode':lookup[f'Exercises/{title}.md'],'toSide':'left','color':'5'})
    cross=[('Nothing Twice','Walk Alone')] if part==2 else [('Nothing Twice','Walk Alone')]
    # Connect concepts across volumes only when the target note exists (the other map retains a local analogue).
    if part==1: cross=[('Half a Day','The Toilet'),('Dreams Realised','The Saga of the Tiffin Carriers'),('Sea-Fever','Waiting for Rain')]
    else: cross=[('The Unstoppable Soul Surfer','A Shield of Courage'),('Toys to Teens','The Ink and the Melody'),('Walk Alone','The Unstoppable Soul Surfer')]
    for a,c in cross: edges.append({'id':f'e{len(edges)+1}','fromNode':lookup[f'Concepts/{a}.md'],'fromSide':'right','toNode':lookup[f'Concepts/{c}.md'],'toSide':'left','color':'6'})
    (BASE/b['canvas']).write_text(json.dumps({'nodes':canv_nodes,'edges':edges},indent=2),encoding='utf-8')

combined=BASE/'Subject Overview.md'
combined.write_text('''---\nclass: 9\nmedium: English\nsubject: English\ntype: overview\ntags: [class/9, medium/english, subject/english, type/overview]\n---\n# English — Class 9 English Medium\n\nThe Kerala Reader comprises two volumes. [[Subject Overview Part 1|Part I]] develops reading, language and composition through Hours and Years, Winds of Change, and Aspire and Explore. [[Subject Overview Part 2|Part II]] continues with Strive and Thrive and Media Matters. Each volume has a separate Canvas and linked lesson notes.\n''',encoding='utf-8')
